 # DriveRepair
 
-### 2026-11-19 partial-repair reporting and USB media safety
+**Diagnose storage problems, rescue readable data, and evaluate whether a drive is safe to reuse.** DriveRepair is a local-first desktop workstation and command-line toolkit for Windows, Linux and selected macOS workflows. It combines read-only diagnosis, image-based recovery, filesystem/file extraction, and *experimental, tightly controlled* HDD maintenance.
 
-Recent Windows traces showed 248 failed **read requests** during a SanDisk SDSSDH3 USB session. A subsequent journaled refresh reported rewritten and readback bytes, but the final operation remained **partial with unresolved data**. The Qt workstation now displays that status and the actual independent postverification result; 100% of planned *steps* is not a healthy-drive certificate. The engine now refuses HDD-style physical writes unless the underlying rotational HDD medium is independently established. A SanDisk SDSSDH3 model label is an advisory SSD classification even when SMART is hidden behind a USB adapter, not a write permit. The read-only device-details UI preserves inventory facts when the optional Windows query fails. See `docs/PARTIAL_REPAIR_TRUTH_AND_USB_IDENTITY_20261119.md`.
+> **Release status:** `0.3.3b18` is a testing build, **not** a hardware-certified repair product. A read-only scan, a repaired filesystem, and an actual physical HDD rewrite are different outcomes. No software can restore bytes that the hardware can no longer return, or guarantee a failing drive's future lifespan.
 
-**Save drives. Save the planet.** Drive diagnostics, image-first rescue, selective file extraction, clone-only filesystem repair and experimental original-media maintenance. Privacy-first and local by default.
+[Get started](#get-started) · [Choose a workflow](#choose-a-workflow) · [Recovery USB](#create-a-recovery-usb) · [Command line](#command-line-examples) · [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting) · [Changelog](CHANGELOG.md) · [Support](https://github.com/sponsors/CommsTech)
 
-> 🌍 **Our mission: Reduce e-waste by extending hardware life.** When a drive "dies," there's usually still data to rescue and life to restore. DriveRepair gives you the surgical tools to breathe new life into storage that's been written off.
+## What DriveRepair does
 
-> **Current source baseline: 0.3.3b14, with post-b14 beta hardening (unreleased). Not approved for unrestricted physical-drive repair.** The supplied source archive is an engineering build. Only the separately built `-public-beta.pyz` package has in-place image/physical-drive writes disabled by distribution policy. Do not distribute the engineering zipapp/wheel as a public safety-qualified release.
+| Workspace | You can do | What it **does not** mean |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **Diagnose** | Read SMART/NVMe health where available, sample or scan drive regions, view observed read delays/errors and a coverage map | A successful read is not proof that the bytes match the original file, or that the disk will remain reliable |
+| **Rescue data** | Copy readable sectors to a **new image on separate healthy storage**, record missing ranges, resume and retry bounded failed reads | A full-size image file is not proof that every byte was successfully recovered |
+| **Targeted HDD repair** | On eligible, positively identified rotational HDDs: inspect weak but repeatably readable regions and perform small, journaled, post-verified rewrites | This cannot regenerate unreadable original bytes, fix mechanical damage, or make SSD/NVMe rewrites into "repairs" |
+| **Analyze recovered images** | Recover select deleted files, carve recognized file formats, check compatible filesystems, work on a clone | File carving cannot reliably reconstruct every fragmented file or encrypted volume |
+| **Create recovery USB** | Flash a separately built DriveRepair hybrid ISO to a **disposable USB drive** with full-image readback | This **erases the USB**. It does not image/repair a patient disk or establish that BIOS/UEFI boot has been tested |
 
-## E-Waste Mission
+**Designed for:** experienced technicians who need evidence, drive owners who want a guided recovery route, and developers testing recovery against synthetic or sacrificial disks. Raw-media repair is currently **engineering/beta only**.
 
-**Why DriveRepair exists:** The electronics industry treats storage devices as disposable. When a drive starts failing, the default response is "replace it." But most failing drives still contain recoverable data and can be restored to functional service.
+## Choose a workflow
 
-DriveRepair exists to change that default. Our toolset is designed to:
-
-- **Rescue data** from failing drives before they're discarded
-- **Extend drive life** through surgical filesystem repair
-- **Verify health** so you know when a drive is truly done
-- **Clone before repair** to preserve the original state
-
-Every drive saved is one less piece of e-waste. Every successful repair is a small victory for the planet.
